p Rate (+ NYC)\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-base font-bold text-foreground\",\"children\":\"10.9% + 3.876%\"}]]}]]}]]}]\n1a:[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"className\":\"text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6\",\"children\":\"Relocation Calculator FAQ\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"space-y-3\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"details\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"group rounded-xl border border-border/30 bg-card/50 overflow-hidden\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"summary\",null,{\"className\":\"flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between gap-3 p-5 text-left font-medium text-foreground hover:bg-muted/10 transition-colors\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm sm:text-base\",\"children\":\"How do I calculate equivalent salary between two states?\"}],[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"className\":\"h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-muted-foreground transition-transform group-open:rotate-180\",\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 24 24\",\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"strokeWidth\":2,\"children\":[\"$\",\"path\",null,{\"strokeLinecap\":\"round\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"d\":\"M19 9l-7 7-7-7\"}]}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"px-5 pb-5 text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\",\"children\":\"You've gotta look at the full picture — state income tax, property taxes, sales taxes, and overall cost of living all factor in. The basic idea is to figure out your current take-home pay after everything, then find the gross salary in the new state that gives you the same net. Our relocation calculator does this automatically so you don't have to crunch all the numbers yourself.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"details\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"group rounded-xl border border-border/30 bg-card/50 overflow-hidden\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"summary\",null,{\"className\":\"flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between gap-3 p-5 text-left font-medium text-foreground hover:bg-muted/10 transition-colors\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm sm:text-base\",\"children\":\"Is moving to a no-income-tax state always better?\"}],[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"className\":\"h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-muted-foreground transition-transform group-open:rotate-180\",\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 24 24\",\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"strokeWidth\":2,\"children\":[\"$\",\"path\",null,{\"strokeLinecap\":\"round\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"d\":\"M19 9l-7 7-7-7\"}]}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"px-5 pb-5 text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\",\"children\":\"Not always. States without income tax still need revenue, so they get it elsewhere — usually through higher property taxes and sales taxes. Texas is the classic example: 0% income tax but a 1.71% average property tax rate, which is nearly double Florida's 0.86%. On a $400K home, that's an extra $3,400 a year in Texas. The bottom line? Look at your total tax bill, not just income tax.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"details\",\"2\",{\"className\":\"group rounded-xl border border-border/30 bg-card/50 overflow-hidden\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"summary\",null,{\"className\":\"flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between gap-3 p-5 text-left font-medium text-foreground hover:bg-muted/10 transition-colors\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm sm:text-base\",\"children\":\"What is cost of living and why does it matter for relocation?\"}],[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"className\":\"h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-muted-foreground transition-transform group-open:rotate-180\",\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 24 24\",\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"strokeWidth\":2,\"children\":[\"$\",\"path\",null,{\"strokeLinecap\":\"round\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"d\":\"M19 9l-7 7-7-7\"}]}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"px-5 pb-5 text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\",\"children\":\"Cost of living is basically how much it costs to maintain your lifestyle in a given place — housing, groceries, healthcare, transportation, all of it. $100K in Texas goes way further than $100K in California because housing costs and taxes are so different. Never compare just the gross salary when you're looking at a relocation offer. The number on paper can be misleading.\"}]]}],\"$L23\",\"$L24\"]}]]}]\n1b:[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-xl border border-border/30 bg-card/50 p-6 sm:p-8\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-lg font-semibold text-foreground mb-4\",\"children\":\"Related Articles\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$Lf\",\"florida-vs-texas-tax-comparison\",{\"href\":\"/blog/florida-vs-texas-tax-comparison\",\"className\":\"group rounded-lg border border-border/30 bg-card/60 p-4 hover:border-emerald-500/30 hover:bg-emerald-500/5 transition-all\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm font-semibold text-foreground group-hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors mb-1\",\"children\":\"Florida vs Texas Tax Comparison\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xs text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed line-clamp-2\",\"children\":\"A detailed comparison of the tax structures in Florida and Texas — two of the most popular no-income-tax states for relocation.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"$Lf\",\"why-texas-has-no-income-tax\",{\"href\":\"/blog/why-texas-has-no-income-tax\",\"className\":\"group rounded-lg border border-border/30 bg-card/60 p-4 hover:border-emerald-500/30 hover:bg-emerald-500/5 transition-all\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm font-semibold text-foreground group-hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors mb-1\",\"children\":\"Why Texas Has No Income Tax\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xs text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed line-clamp-2\",\"children\":\"How Texas funds its government without a personal income tax, and what it means for residents in terms of property taxes and overall tax burden.\"}]]}]]}]]}]\n1c:[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-xl 